      <a name="style" val="p-margin: 0px;"/>
      <a name="text" val="pull-down resistor ensures the bus is pulled down to 0 if no output is driving to 1"/>
      <a name="textwidth" val="267"/>
      <a name="valign" val="base"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="0" loc="(170,350)" name="Callout">
      <a name="background" val="#b7e1eb71"/>
      <a name="dx" val="-20"/>
      <a name="dy" val="-20"/>
      <a name="foreground" val="#236494"/>
      <a name="format" val="wrapped"/>
      <a name="halign" val="right"/>
      <a name="style" val="p-margin: 0px;"/>
      <a name="text" val="push-pull output is always driving"/>
      <a name="textwidth" val="111"/>
      <a name="valign" val="top"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="0" loc="(192,821)" name="Text">
      <a name="font" val="SansSerif bold 12"/>
      <a name="foreground" val="#236494"/>
      <a name="halign" val="center"/>
      <a name="text" val="OR"/>
      <a name="valign" val="base"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="0" loc="(20,20)" name="Text">
      <a name="background" val="#87eb675e"/>
      <a name="format" val="markdownish"/>
      <a name="style" val="margin: 0.5em; h1 { accent: underline 0.25em; }; h2 { accent: block 0.75em #3dd;}"/>
      <a name="text"># Common Gates - Advanced Features

## Output Driver Option

Normally, gates use **push-pull** outputs: they actively output either 1 or 0 at all times, as long as the inputs are well defined. The ***Output Driver*** attribute allows for two other behaviors. 
* With a **pull-up-only** output driver (equivalent to open-emitter/open-source) a gate can actively output 1, but instead of outputting 0 it will produce a floating (z) output. 
* With **pull-down-only** output driver (equivalent to open-collector/open-drain), a gate can actively output 0, but instead of outputting 1 it will produce a floating (z) output. 
This allows creation of *wired-OR* and *wired-AND* busses. ***Pull Resistor*** (found in the ***Analog*** library) can be useful for these too.</a>
      <a name="textwidth" val="480"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="0" loc="(20,670)" name="Text">
      <a name="background" val="#87eb675e"/>
      <a name="format" val="markdownish"/>
      <a name="style" val="margin: 0.5em; h1 { accent: underline 0.25em; }; h2 { accent: block 0.75em #3dd;}"/>
      <a name="text">## Gate Appearance and International Standards

Different standards bodies have adopted different symbols for  common gates. In **Logisim &gt; Settings &gt; International** you can choose from **ANSI/IEEE Standard 91a-1991** (curved, distinctive shapes commonly used in computer science textbooks and educational materials), **IEC 60617-12** (rectangular, uniform shapes often used in professional settings and engineering), and **DIN 40700** (an older, less common standard).</a>
      <a name="textwidth" val="480"/>
    </comp>
    <comp lib="0" loc="(231,544)" name="Callout">
