  <circuit name="Common Gates - Reference">
    <comp lib="0" loc="(20,20)" name="Text">
      <a name="background" val="#87eb675e"/>
      <a name="format" val="markdownish"/>
      <a name="style" val="margin: 0.5em; h1 { accent: underline 0.25em; }; h2 { accent: block 0.75em #3dd;}"/>
      <a name="text"># Common Gates - Reference

## Properties

Common multiple-input logic gates, AND, OR, NAND, NOR, XOR, XNOR, support these properties:

| Property      | Values                       | Notes
|:--------      |:---------                    |:----------
| Facing        | East, North, West, South     | Orientation of the output port.
| Data Bits     | 1-32                         | Width of each input and output port. If set 
+               |                              | to *N*, the gate outputs *N* values, with 
+               |                              | each output value computed from the 
+               |                              | corresponding bit of the input ports.
| Gate Size     | Narrow, Medium, Wide         | Size of the gate symbol.
| Number of     | 2-32                         | Up to 32 inputs are allowed.
+    Inputs     |                              | Unconnected inputs are ignored.
| Output Driver | 0/1 (push-pull),&lt;br&gt;         | Determines the set of possible output values,
+               | 0/floating (pull-down-only), | in other words, if the output port is capable
+               | &lt;br&gt;floating/1 (pull-up-only)| of actively driving high values, low values,
+               |                              | or both.
| Label         | any text                     | Text to be shown on top of the gate.
| Label Font    | any font                     | Font to be used for the label.
| Negate *i*    | Yes, No                      | Whether to invert the value of input port 
+               |                              | *i*. Negated inputs are drawn with a "bubble"
+               |                              | symbol at the port.
| Multiple-Input| When an odd number are on,   | For XOR and XNOR gates with more than two 
+ Behavior      | &lt;br&gt;When one input is on     | inputs, chooses between parity-style 
+               |                              | behavior and one-hot behavior.

## Keyboard Shortcuts

+ &#128073;Arrow keys (↑→↓←) change the *Facing* property for the selected gate or gates.

+ &#128073;Typing a number (2-32) changes the *Number of Inputs* property for the 
  selected gate or gates.

+ &#128073;Typing a number (1-32) while holding the option key (⌥) or Alt key changes the *Data Bits*
   property for the selected gate or gates.


## Behavior

* **AND** Gate
  - Unconnected inputs are ignored.
  - If there are no connected inputs, outputs error (e).
  - Outputs 1 when *all* connected inputs are 1 (and there are some connected inputs).
  - Outputs 0 when *any* input is 0.
  - In all other cases, outputs error (e). This includes cases where some inputs are floating
    (z) or errors (e), unless some input is 0, in which case the above rule applies and the
    output is 0.

* **NAND** Gate
  - Outputs 1, 0, or e, whenever AND would have outputted 0, 1, or e, respectively.

* **OR** Gate
  - Unconnected inputs are ignored.
  - If there are no connected inputs, outputs error (e).
  - Outputs 1 when *any* input is 1.
  - Outputs 0 when *all* connected inputs are 0 (and there are some connected inputs).
  - In all other cases, outputs error (e). This includes cases where some inputs are floating
    (z) or errors (e), unless some input is 1, in which case the above rule applies and the
    output is 1.

* **NOR** Gate
  - Outputs 1, 0, or e, whenever OR would have outputted 0, 1, or e, respectively.

* **XOR** Gate with Multiple-Input Behavior set to "When an odd number are on"
  - Unconnected inputs are ignored.
  - If there are no connected inputs, outputs error (e).
  - Outputs 1 when an odd number of inputs are 1, and all other connected inputs
    are 0.
  - Outputs 0 when an even number of inputs are 1, and all other connected inputs
    are 0 (and there are some connected inputs).
  - In all other cases, outputs error (e). This includes all cases where some inputs are 
    floating (z) or errors (e).

* **XOR** Gate with Multiple-Input Behavior set to "When one input is on"
  - Unconnected inputs are ignored.
  - If there are no connected inputs, outputs error (e).
  - Outputs 1 when exactly one input is 1, and all other connected inputs
    are 0.
  - Outputs 0 when either zero or more than two connected inputs are 1, and all
    other connected inputs are 0 (and there are some connected inputs).
  - In all other cases, outputs error (e). This includes all cases where some inputs are 
    floating (z) or errors (e).

* **XNOR** Gate
  - Outputs 1, 0, or e, whenever XOR would have outputted 0, 1, or e, respectively.
</a>
      <a name="textwidth" val="700"/>
    </comp>
  </circuit>
